<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      NETWORK CARVE-OUTS. A censor-resistant tunnel is only useful while the
      device has a network to carry it, so these are normal (install-time,
      non-dangerous) permissions pinned to the app rather than left to default:
        - INTERNET: the socket the VpnService routes through. Without it the
          tunnel cannot egress anything. Always present.
        - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE / ACCESS_WIFI_STATE: the datapath reads the
          live network state (interface up/down, transport type, default
          route) to decide whether a tunnel that is up is actually carrying
          traffic. Read-only; no location, no coarse position derivation.
      These two (plus the forbidden ones below) are the full runtime surface
      the disguise exposes in Settings > Apps > (this app) > Permissions.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_WIFI_STATE" />
    <!--
      FOREGROUND_SERVICE + FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE: the tunnel runs as a
      foreground service (the only route that can survive for hours unattended
      on modern Android), declared with foregroundServiceType="specialUse" on
      the VpnTunnelService below and justified there via
      android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE. POST_NOTIFICATIONS lets the
      running-tunnel foreground notification be shown on API 33+ (it is
      requested at runtime, never assumed). WAKE_LOCK keeps the CPU up for the
      datapath while the screen is off. A tunnel that sleeps is a tunnel a
      censor's network jamming simply waits out.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!--
      BATTERY_EXEMPTION CONTRACT. REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS is the
      platform hook that lets this app ask the user to be excluded from Doze /
      App Standby, so a running tunnel is not frozen mid-session. For a
      censored user a tunnel silently frozen by the battery optimizer is
      indistinguishable from the censor cutting the connection. It is a
      NORMAL permission (automatically granted, no runtime prompt); the code
      that must translate it into a user-visible request is
      VpnTunnelService, and it is merely an ask. Doze is never bypassed, the
      user always approves it in the system battery screen.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS" />
    <!--
      SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Declaring it would put
      "Display over other apps" in the Settings permission list and trigger
      Play's restricted-permission review; nothing in this app renders a
      TYPE_APPLICATION_OVERLAY window on any platform (panic/duress paths all
      run inside the normal activity/frame). Adding an unused dangerous
      permission would be a fingerprint the disguise exists to avoid.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
    <!--
      OI-9 (Round 36 treaty): QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES is retained ONLY because the
      split-tunnel picker enumerates the full installed-application inventory
      (loadInstalledAppsForSplitTunnel uses getInstalledApplications, including
      non-launching system apps a <queries> LAUNCHER filter cannot surface).
      Play distribution requires a Permissions Declaration Form asserting this
      core-functionality use; that declaration is an operator action and must
      be completed before the next Play submission. The <queries> entries below
      keep browser and launcher visibility correct even if this permission is
      ever narrowed or revoked.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES"
        tools:ignore="QueryAllPackagesPermission" />

    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="http" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!--
      THE APPLICATION LABEL IS PERMANENTLY THE DISGUISE, AND IT HAS TO BE.

      This attribute is resolved by the package manager at INSTALL time, into
      ApplicationInfo.nonLocalizedLabel, and cached there for the life of the
      install. Nothing in the SDK changes it afterwards: PackageManager
      exposes getApplicationLabel with no counterpart setter, and a javap
      sweep of every public class in android.content.pm, android.content.om
      and android.app on API 36 finds no label setter that reaches an
      INSTALLED package. TaskDescription.Builder, ShortcutInfo.Builder,
      RemoteInput.Builder, PackageInstaller.SessionParams.setAppLabel and
      PreapprovalDetails.Builder each label something else (a Recents card, a
      launcher shortcut, an inline reply, an install session, an install
      prompt). Because the value below is a LITERAL rather than an @string
      reference it is stored as nonLocalizedLabel with labelRes == 0, so
      there is not even a resource id for a runtime resource overlay to
      retarget. The latched-surface pattern the VPN session name and the two
      notification channels use (read the camouflage state, rename on the
      next Activity start) therefore cannot be applied to this attribute at
      all. It cannot FOLLOW the disguise; it can only BE the disguise.

      What it feeds is the widest surface this app has, and none of it needs
      a tunnel, a notification, or any user action to be visible:

        Settings > Apps: the list row, and the App info title
        Settings > Apps > (this app) > Notifications: the SCREEN TITLE over
          the very channel rows two earlier lanes disguised
        Settings > Network and internet > VPN
        the "wants to set up a VPN connection" consent dialog
        the POST_NOTIFICATIONS runtime permission prompt
        the app-name byline Android draws above every notification we post
        battery, storage, data-usage and permission-manager entries

      That is what somebody who already suspects the owner of this phone
      reads first, and it was the last surface still naming the product.

      The un-camouflaged identity is carried instead by the .LauncherReal
      activity-alias below, which overrides this label for the launcher entry:
      the same mechanism .LauncherDecoy already uses, applied to the other
      state. The two configurations are:

        camouflage ON  -> home screen decoy name, Settings decoy name
        camouflage OFF -> home screen real name,  Settings decoy name

      The asymmetry is real and is confined to the second row: a user who has
      opted OUT of camouflage sees the product on the home screen and the
      decoy in Settings, and pays mild confusion for it. Before this change
      the asymmetry landed on the FIRST row instead, on the users relying on
      the disguise, and they paid disclosure. An app that is never
      identifiable in the app list is worth more than a tidy Settings entry.

      Pinned to VpnTunnelService.DISGUISED_DISPLAY_NAME and to the
      .LauncherDecoy alias by CamouflageLatchedSurfaceTest, which parses this
      file. The Play Store LISTING name is a separate thing and is not this.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="Calculator"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <meta-data
                android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
                android:resource="@style/NormalTheme" />
        </activity>

        <activity
            android:name=".RealLauncherActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:taskAffinity="org.winsvpn.app.launcher"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme" />

        <activity
            android:name=".DecoyLauncherActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:taskAffinity="org.winsvpn.app.launcher"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme" />

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".LauncherReal"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
            android:label="Win's VPN"
            android:targetActivity=".RealLauncherActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".LauncherDecoy"
            android:enabled="true"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_calculator"
            android:label="Calculator"
            android:targetActivity=".DecoyLauncherActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
          THE VPN BINDING CONTRACT. This is the one declaration the platform
          enforces: a service with android:permission="android.permission.BIND_VPN_SERVICE"
          and an <action android:name="android.net.VpnService"/> intent-filter is
          the ONLY shape the system's VpnService grant accepts. That pair gives us
          everything else:
            - the permission attribute restricts who may call bindService to the
              system alone, so no third party can bind this tunnel. That is
              why android:exported="false" is also safe and correct (the system
              is exempt from exported gating for this permissioned binding);
            - the intent-filter is what VpnService.prepare() and the consent
              dialog key on to identify the app a user is about to trust;
            - foregroundServiceType="specialUse" + the PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE
              justify the running-tunnel foreground service to API 34+.
          The wrapping app complicates the label but never this mapping: the
          disguise changes the visible NAME (see the application label note
          above), not the class that owns the tunnel.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".VpnTunnelService"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_VPN_SERVICE"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse"
            android:stopWithTask="false"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.net.VpnService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Maintains the user-requested Win's VPN encrypted network tunnel." />
        </service>

        <!-- The bundled AWG library declares its own backend service. This app uses the
             audited service above, so remove the unused duplicate VpnService. -->
        <service
            android:name="org.amnezia.awg.backend.GoBackend$VpnService"
            tools:node="remove" />

        <!-- OI-10 (Round 36 treaty): the exported Telephony.SECRET_CODE receiver was
             removed. The dialer code *#*#9467#*#* fingerprinted the covert app in an
             extractable manifest; covert entry stays inside the calculator digit
             sequence only. -->

        <!-- Wear OS companion. Must be exported because Google Play services
             binds it on behalf of the paired watch; the Data Layer only routes
             between nodes running this same package signed with this same
             certificate, and the path filter keeps the surface to one route. -->
        <service
            android:name=".WatchCommandService"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED" />
                <data
                    android:scheme="wear"
                    android:host="*"
                    android:pathPrefix="/winsvpn/cmd" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />

        <!--
          STRIP THE LIBRARY-ADDED PROFILEINSTALL RECEIVER.

          androidx.profileinstaller (pulled in transitively by the Flutter /
          AndroidX stack) declares its own ProfileInstallReceiver as
          android:exported="true", protected only by the platform's
          android.permission.DUMP signature grant. The exported-component
          whitelist in AndroidExportedComponentAndVpnPermissionContractTest
          treats THIS file plus the tv variant as the whole exported surface,
          but the receiver is merged in by Gradle and therefore never appears
          in either source manifest. So the contract passed while the
          produced release bytes exported a component the app neither declares
          nor needs. Removing it by the exact name the AAR declares closes the
          gap: what the merged release manifest exports is again exactly the
          justified set. The app does not ship baseline profiles, so the
          receiver has nothing to do.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="androidx.profileinstaller.ProfileInstallReceiver"
            tools:ignore="MissingClass"
            tools:node="remove" />
    </application>
</manifest>
